-- scripts/dst/corpus_pr_dst.ail
--
-- WI-A15 commit 1 acceptance, under ADR-001 D11: THE BLOCKING PR CORPUS.
--
-- ============================================================================
-- WHAT THIS SUITE CLAIMS
-- ============================================================================
--
-- That the fixed bank is REAL: every member is a program this run generated,
-- persisted and loaded back, keyed on both of the corpus's keys, carrying the
-- execution manifest out of its own bytes — and that the bank COLLECTIVELY
-- reaches every required non-waived fault class a seed can reach, with the ones
-- no seed can reach recorded as coverage findings rather than waived away.
--
-- ============================================================================
-- THE SEEDS ARE A QUERY'S ANSWER, NOT AN AUTHOR'S LIST
-- ============================================================================
--
-- 260 seeds were swept through the real driver and the bank is the union of
-- five DERIVED selections. Every member therefore has an asserted reason, and a
-- change to the generator, to a request projection string or to the driver's
-- control flow moves it and fails loudly:
--
--   1, 176      the shortest and longest eligible trajectories (n = 9 and 50).
--               S8's complement: a bank that walks only the middle certifies
--               only the middle, and the paths it does not walk are not pinned
--               but ABSENT.
--   2, 3        the two smallest seeds reaching approval_denied.
--   6, 20       the two smallest reaching ToolCorrelationMismatch.
--   14, 15      the two smallest reaching ToolDeadlineExceeded.
--   23, 39      the two smallest reaching ToolFailed.
--   114         the richest Err-terminating trajectory (3 classes). It enters
--               as a PROMOTED REGRESSION, so the bank carries D11's second kind
--               and not only its first.
--   195         THE ONLY SEED OF 260 that reaches all four sweep-reachable
--               classes by itself. One in 260 is why the bank is a query.
--
-- Two witnesses per class rather than one, deliberately: a bank with a single
-- witness for a class loses that class to any generator drift that moves one
-- seed, and loses it SILENTLY, because the remaining members still pass.
--
-- ============================================================================
-- THE MEASUREMENT THAT CHANGED THE OBSERVER, AND IT IS SITE 25's SHAPE
-- ============================================================================
--
-- The obvious class observer is `Interaction.outcome.fault_class_id`. It is the
-- right quantity and it is INSUFFICIENT ON ITS OWN: `ports.tool_outcome_record`
-- is the only writer, so the field can only ever name the three TOOL classes.
-- A sweep graded by it reports "0 of 260 seeds reach approval_denied" while 152
-- of them do.
--
-- So the class observer is three observers, each named at its site:
--
--   the three tool classes        `outcome.fault_class_id`
--   approval_denied               the approval interaction's PAYLOAD, which is
--                                 the operator's raw answer — `{"type":"deny"}`
--   provider_empty_terminal_      the provider interaction's decoded outcome:
--   response                      empty prose, "stop", no tool calls, which is
--                                 exactly `session.c2_loop`'s guard
--                                 `trim(info.output) == ""`
--
-- AND THE BRANCH OBSERVER IS NOT IN THIS PROCESS AT ALL. `NativeToolDenied` and
-- `NativeToolResults` are both in `dst_invariants.d64_gap_register()` — they do
-- not reach the returned trace — so a branch-reached counter read in here would
-- be reading the recorder that wrote the log. The branch witness is the WIRE,
-- and `make corpus_pr` counts it from production code that knows nothing about
-- the interaction log. That is why D11 makes them separate counters: they have
-- different observers, and only one of them is independent.
--
-- ============================================================================
-- WHAT NO SEED CAN REACH, AND WHY THAT IS A FINDING RATHER THAN A WAIVER
-- ============================================================================
--
-- Nine required classes are not waived by `driver_only`. Measured over 260
-- seeds, FOUR are reachable and five are not, in four different ways:
--
--   provider_error_retryable          `ScriptedStep` has no error channel.
--   provider_error_non_retryable      Same field; one named change away.
--   provider_protocol_inconsistent_   the generator's tool_args are always
--   result                            well-formed JSON.
--   provider_partial_stream_then_     the deterministic adapters this corpus
--   error                             drives report emissions: [] even when
--                                     they play chunks, and nothing consumes
--                                     the field. NO LONGER externally blocked:
--                                     WI-C1 adopted stepWithStreamRecorded and
--                                     `live_ports` returns the real log. Ours
--                                     now, and WI-C3's.
--   provider_empty_terminal_response  UNREACHABLE BY SEARCH and reachable by
--                                     CONSTRUCTION: every generated terminal
--                                     step carries non-empty prose
--                                     (`gp${draws}-${arg}`).
--
-- The last is cluster 12's limit arriving where the plan predicted its SHAPE
-- and not its INSTANCE — the plan named the provider fault class; the
-- measurement adds this one. It is covered by a CONSTRUCTED member, and the
-- construction's justification is checkable: `ConstructedForReachableClass`
-- fires the moment a swept seed reaches it, so the register can only shrink.
--
-- None of the five is a waiver. D3 requires a waived class to be named with its
-- waiving condition, and a class nobody waived that nobody reached is a
-- coverage gap, not a permission.

